ion-colors line-clamp-2 mb-1.5\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"OBBBA Tax Refund Impact 2026: How the New Tax Law Changes Your Refund\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-xs text-muted-foreground line-clamp-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"See exactly how the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (OBBBA) changes your 2026 tax refund. New SALT cap, doubled Child Tax Credit, tip/overtime deductions, and worked examples comparing OBBBA vs prior law.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-1\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"OBBBA 2026\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"OBBBA 2026\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"One Big Beautiful Bill Act\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"One Big Beautiful Bill Act\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"OBBBA tax refund\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"OBBBA tax refund\\\"}]]}]]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L11\\\",\\\"2026-self-employed-tax-refund-calculator\\\",{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/blog/2026-self-employed-tax-refund-calculator\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-card p-5 hover:border-emerald-500/30 hover:shadow-md transition-all\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"flex items-center gap-2 mb-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border px-2 py-0.5 text-[10px] font-medium bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800 dark:bg-emerald-500/20 dark:text-emerald-400 border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-500/30\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Tax Guide\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"July 4, 2026\\\"}]]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"h3\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-sm font-semibold text-foreground group-hover:text-emerald-600 dark:group-hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors line-clamp-2 mb-1.5\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Self-Employed Tax Refund Calculator 2026: SE Tax, Quarterly Payments \\u0026 Deductions\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"text-xs text-muted-foreground line-clamp-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Complete 2026 tax refund guide for self-employed workers, freelancers, and contractors. Calculate SE tax, quarterly estimated payments, business deductions, and your expected refund with worked examples.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-1\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"self-employed tax refund 2026\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"self-employed tax refund 2026\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",\\\"self-employment tax calculator\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"self-employment tax calculator\\\"}],\\\"$L2c\\\"]}]]}],\\\"$L2d\\\"]}]]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"2e:T26c3,\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"\\u003cp\\u003eHave you ever looked at a receipt and wondered, \\\"How much did this item actually cost before tax?\\\" That's exactly what a reverse sales tax calculator does. Instead of adding tax to a price, it works backwards from the total (tax-inclusive) price to find the original pre-tax amount and the tax you paid.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThis guide explains how reverse sales tax works, the formula behind it, and how to use it for any U.S. state.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"what-is-a-reverse-sales-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003eWhat Is a Reverse Sales Tax Calculator?\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eA standard sales tax calculator takes a pre-tax price and adds tax to get the total. A \\u003cstrong\\u003ereverse sales tax calculator\\u003c/strong\\u003e does the opposite:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eInput\\u003c/strong\\u003e: The total amount you paid (including tax)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eOutput\\u003c/strong\\u003e: The original price (before tax) and the tax amount\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThis is useful for:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eAccounting and bookkeeping\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Separating tax from total receipts\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eExpense reports\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Logging pre-tax amounts for reimbursement\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eBudgeting\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Understanding the true cost of purchases\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eBusiness invoicing\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Itemizing tax on tax-inclusive pricing\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"the-reverse-sales-tax-formula\\\"\\u003eThe Reverse Sales Tax Formula\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe formula to calculate the pre-tax price from a total is:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cpre\\u003e\\u003ccode\\u003ePre-Tax Price = Total Price ÷ (1 + Sales Tax Rate)\\u003c/code\\u003e\\u003c/pre\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eAnd to find the tax amount:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cpre\\u003e\\u003ccode\\u003eTax Amount = Total Price − Pre-Tax Price\\u003c/code\\u003e\\u003c/pre\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"example-calculation\\\"\\u003eExample Calculation\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eLet's say you paid \\u003cstrong\\u003e$108.25\\u003c/strong\\u003e total in a city with an \\u003cstrong\\u003e8.25%\\u003c/strong\\u003e combined sales tax rate.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eStep 1: Convert the percentage to a decimal\\u003c/strong\\u003e 8.25% = 0.0825\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eStep 2: Add 1 to the tax rate\\u003c/strong\\u003e 1 + 0.0825 = 1.0825\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eStep 3: Divide the total by this number\\u003c/strong\\u003e $108.25 ÷ 1.0825 = \\u003cstrong\\u003e$100.00\\u003c/strong\\u003e (Pre-Tax Price)\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eStep 4: Subtract to find the tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e $108.25 − $100.00 = \\u003cstrong\\u003e$8.25\\u003c/strong\\u003e (Tax Amount)\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSo, the item cost $100.00 before tax, and you paid $8.25 in sales tax.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"reverse-sales-tax-by-state\\\"\\u003eReverse Sales Tax by State\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSales tax rates vary widely across the United States. Here are examples of reverse tax calculations for a \\u003cstrong\\u003e$100 total purchase\\u003c/strong\\u003e in different states:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"table-wrapper\\\"\\u003e\\u003ctable\\u003e\\u003cthead\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eState\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eCombined Rate\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003ePre-Tax Price\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eTax Amount\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eCalifornia (avg)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e8.82%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$91.89\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$8.11\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eNew York (avg)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e8.52%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$92.15\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$7.85\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eTexas (avg)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e8.20%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$92.42\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$7.58\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eIllinois (avg)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e8.81%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$91.90\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$8.10\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eFlorida (avg)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e7.02%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$93.44\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$6.56\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eGeorgia (avg)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e7.35%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$93.16\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$6.84\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eWashington (avg)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e9.00%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$91.74\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$8.26\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eTennessee (avg)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e9.55%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$91.28\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$8.72\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003eOregon\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e0.00%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$100.00\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$0.00\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eUse our \\u003ca href=\\\"/sales-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003esales tax calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e to find exact rates for any state or city.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"states-with-no-sales-tax\\\"\\u003eStates with No Sales Tax\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf you live in a state with no sales tax, reverse tax calculation is simple: the pre-tax price equals the total price. Five states have no state-level sales tax:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003col\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eOregon\\u003c/strong\\u003e — No state sales tax\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eMontana\\u003c/strong\\u003e — No state sales tax\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eNew Hampshire\\u003c/strong\\u003e — No state sales tax\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eDelaware\\u003c/strong\\u003e — No state sales tax\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eAlaska\\u003c/strong\\u003e — No state sales tax (but local cities may charge local sales tax)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"how-to-use-a-reverse-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003eHow to Use a Reverse Tax Calculator\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003col\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eFind your total amount\\u003c/strong\\u003e — Look at your receipt or invoice for the grand total\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eKnow your sales tax rate\\u003c/strong\\u003e — This includes state + local + special district taxes\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eEnter the total and rate\\u003c/strong\\u003e into the calculator\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eGet your results\\u003c/strong\\u003e — The calculator shows you the pre-tax price and the tax paid\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ol\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"finding-your-local-sales-tax-rate\\\"\\u003eFinding Your Local Sales Tax Rate\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSales tax isn't just the state rate — it's the combined rate of:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eState sales tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eCounty sales tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eCity sales tax\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eSpecial district taxes\\u003c/strong\\u003e (transit, stadium, etc.)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFor example, in Chicago, Illinois:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eState: 6.25%\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eCounty: 1.75%\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eCity: 1.25%\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eSpecial: 0.50%\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eTotal\\u003c/strong\\u003e: 9.75%\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eYou can find your exact local rate using our \\u003ca href=\\\"/sales-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003esales tax calculator by state\\u003c/a\\u003e.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"reverse-tax-for-businesses\\\"\\u003eReverse Tax for Businesses\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf you run a business, reverse tax calculations are essential for:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"1-sales-tax-filing\\\"\\u003e1. Sales Tax Filing\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWhen filing your monthly or quarterly sales tax return, you need to report total sales and total tax collected. If your POS system tracks tax-inclusive totals, you'll need to reverse-calculate to separate the tax.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"2-expense-tracking\\\"\\u003e2. Expense Tracking\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWhen you buy supplies for your business, receipts often show only the total. To deduct the actual expense (not the tax) on your Schedule C or corporate return, you need the pre-tax amount.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"3-pricing-strategy\\\"\\u003e3. Pricing Strategy\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf you advertise \\\"tax-included\\\" pricing (common at festivals, food trucks, and some retail), you need to know how much tax is embedded in each sale to remit to the state.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"4-receipt-auditing\\\"\\u003e4. Receipt Auditing\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf you're auditing receipts for accuracy, reverse tax calculation helps verify that the correct rate was applied.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"reverse-sales-tax-calculator-tool\\\"\\u003eReverse Sales Tax Calculator Tool\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eUse this simple tool to calculate the pre-tax price from any total amount:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"quick-reference-table\\\"\\u003eQuick Reference Table\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eHere's a quick reference for a \\u003cstrong\\u003e$100 total purchase\\u003c/strong\\u003e at common tax rates:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cdiv class=\\\"table-wrapper\\\"\\u003e\\u003ctable\\u003e\\u003cthead\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eTax Rate\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003ePre-Tax Price\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003cth\\u003eTax Amount\\u003c/th\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e5.00%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$95.24\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$4.76\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e6.00%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$94.34\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$5.66\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e6.25%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$94.12\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$5.88\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e7.00%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$93.46\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$6.54\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e7.25%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$93.24\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$6.76\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e7.50%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$93.02\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$6.98\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e8.00%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$92.59\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$7.41\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e8.25%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$92.38\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$7.62\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e8.50%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$92.17\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$7.83\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e9.00%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$91.74\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$8.26\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e9.50%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$91.32\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$8.68\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e10.00%\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$90.91\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003ctd\\u003e$9.09\\u003c/td\\u003e\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\u003c/div\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"common-mistakes-in-reverse-tax-calculation\\\"\\u003eCommon Mistakes in Reverse Tax Calculation\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"mistake-1-subtracting-the-percentage\\\"\\u003eMistake 1: Subtracting the Percentage\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eWrong\\u003c/strong\\u003e: $108.25 − (8.25% of $108.25) = $99.33 \\u003cstrong\\u003eCorrect\\u003c/strong\\u003e: $108.25 ÷ 1.0825 = $100.00\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eYou cannot simply subtract the tax percentage from the total. You must divide by (1 + rate).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"mistake-2-using-the-wrong-rate\\\"\\u003eMistake 2: Using the Wrong Rate\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eMake sure you're using the \\u003cstrong\\u003ecombined\\u003c/strong\\u003e sales tax rate (state + local), not just the state rate. Using only the state rate will give you an incorrect pre-tax amount.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch3 id=\\\"mistake-3-rounding-errors\\\"\\u003eMistake 3: Rounding Errors\\u003c/h3\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eAlways calculate to at least 4 decimal places before rounding to cents. Rounding too early can cause penny-off discrepancies.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"frequently-asked-questions\\\"\\u003eFrequently Asked Questions\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eHow do I calculate reverse sales tax manually?\\u003c/strong\\u003e Divide the total price by (1 + tax rate as a decimal). For example, for a $50 total at 7% tax: $50 ÷ 1.07 = $46.73 pre-tax price.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eCan I use reverse tax for VAT (Value Added Tax)?\\u003c/strong\\u003e Yes. The formula is the same. For example, with 20% VAT: £120 ÷ 1.20 = £100 pre-tax.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhy doesn't subtracting the percentage work?\\u003c/strong\\u003e Because the total includes tax, calculating the percentage of the total gives you a smaller number than the actual tax. The tax was calculated on the pre-tax price, not the total.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eWhat if I don't know my local tax rate?\\u003c/strong\\u003e Use our \\u003ca href=\\\"/sales-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003esales tax calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e to look up rates for any U.S. city or county.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eAre reverse tax calculations exact?\\u003c/strong\\u003e Yes, mathematically. However, rounding to the nearest cent can sometimes cause a 1¢ difference. This is normal and acceptable for accounting purposes.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"related-resources\\\"\\u003eRelated Resources\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/sales-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003eSales Tax Calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e — Calculate sales tax for any state\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/blog/sales-tax-by-state-guide-2026\\\"\\u003eSales Tax by State Guide\\u003c/a\\u003e — Complete rate reference\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/property-tax-calculator\\\"\\u003eProperty Tax Calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e — Estimate property tax\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\\"/paycheck-calculator\\\"\\u003ePaycheck Calculator\\u003c/a\\u003e — Take-home pay after income tax\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003ch2 id=\\\"sources\\\"\\u003eSources\\u003c/h2\\u003e\\u003col\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eTax Foundation — State and Local Sales Tax Rates (2026)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eIRS — Publication 525 (Taxable and Nontaxable Income)\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eState Revenue Departments — Local Sales Tax Rate Tables\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eU.S. Census Bureau — Quarterly Summary of State and Local Tax Revenue\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ol\\u003e\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"2a:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mx-auto max-w-3xl lg:mx-0 lg:flex-1\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"prose-container\\\",\\\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\\\":{\\\"__html\\\":\\\"$2e\\\"}}]}]\\n2b:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mx-