\"children\":\"Tax Guide\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\",\"children\":\"July 4, 2026\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm font-semibold text-foreground group-hover:text-emerald-600 dark:group-hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors line-clamp-2 mb-1.5\",\"children\":\"OBBBA Tax Refund Impact 2026: How the New Tax Law Changes Your Refund\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xs text-muted-foreground line-clamp-2\",\"children\":\"See exactly how the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (OBBBA) changes your 2026 tax refund. New SALT cap, doubled Child Tax Credit, tip/overtime deductions, and worked examples comparing OBBBA vs prior law.\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-1\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"OBBBA 2026\",{\"className\":\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\",\"children\":\"OBBBA 2026\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",\"One Big Beautiful Bill Act\",{\"className\":\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\",\"children\":\"One Big Beautiful Bill Act\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",\"OBBBA tax refund\",{\"className\":\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\",\"children\":\"OBBBA tax refund\"}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"$L3\",\"2026-self-employed-tax-refund-calculator\",{\"href\":\"/blog/2026-self-employed-tax-refund-calculator\",\"className\":\"group rounded-xl border border-border/50 bg-card p-5 hover:border-emerald-500/30 hover:shadow-md transition-all\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex items-center gap-2 mb-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border px-2 py-0.5 text-[10px] font-medium bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800 dark:bg-emerald-500/20 dark:text-emerald-400 border-emerald-300 dark:border-emerald-500/30\",\"children\":\"Tax Guide\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"text-[10px] text-muted-foreground\",\"children\":\"July 4, 2026\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"className\":\"text-sm font-semibold text-foreground group-hover:text-emerald-600 dark:group-hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors line-clamp-2 mb-1.5\",\"children\":\"Self-Employed Tax Refund Calculator 2026: SE Tax, Quarterly Payments & Deductions\"}],[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xs text-muted-foreground line-clamp-2\",\"children\":\"Complete 2026 tax refund guide for self-employed workers, freelancers, and contractors. Calculate SE tax, quarterly estimated payments, business deductions, and your expected refund with worked examples.\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-1\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",\"self-employed tax refund 2026\",{\"className\":\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\",\"children\":\"self-employed tax refund 2026\"}],[\"$\",\"span\",\"self-employment tax calculator\",{\"className\":\"inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-border/30 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[9px] text-muted-foreground\",\"children\":\"self-employment tax calculator\"}],\"$L12\"]}]]}],\"$L13\"]}]]}]\nd:[\"$\",\"script\",\"script-0\",{\"src\":\"/_next/static/chunks/2d86c01f2aacd453.js\",\"async\":true}]\ne:[\"$\",\"$L14\",null,{\"children\":[\"$\",\"$15\",null,{\"name\":\"Next.MetadataOutlet\",\"children\":\"$@16\"}]}]\n17:T26c3,<p>Have you ever looked at a receipt and wondered, \"How much did this item actually cost before tax?\" That's exactly what a reverse sales tax calculator does. Instead of adding tax to a price, it works backwards from the total (tax-inclusive) price to find the original pre-tax amount and the tax you paid.</p><p>This guide explains how reverse sales tax works, the formula behind it, and how to use it for any U.S. state.</p><h2 id=\"what-is-a-reverse-sales-tax-calculator\">What Is a Reverse Sales Tax Calculator?</h2><p>A standard sales tax calculator takes a pre-tax price and adds tax to get the total. A <strong>reverse sales tax calculator</strong> does the opposite:</p><ul><li><strong>Input</strong>: The total amount you paid (including tax)</li><li><strong>Output</strong>: The original price (before tax) and the tax amount</li></ul><p>This is useful for:</p><ul><li><strong>Accounting and bookkeeping</strong> — Separating tax from total receipts</li><li><strong>Expense reports</strong> — Logging pre-tax amounts for reimbursement</li><li><strong>Budgeting</strong> — Understanding the true cost of purchases</li><li><strong>Business invoicing</strong> — Itemizing tax on tax-inclusive pricing</li></ul><h2 id=\"the-reverse-sales-tax-formula\">The Reverse Sales Tax Formula</h2><p>The formula to calculate the pre-tax price from a total is:</p><pre><code>Pre-Tax Price = Total Price ÷ (1 + Sales Tax Rate)</code></pre><p>And to find the tax amount:</p><pre><code>Tax Amount = Total Price − Pre-Tax Price</code></pre><h3 id=\"example-calculation\">Example Calculation</h3><p>Let's say you paid <strong>$108.25</strong> total in a city with an <strong>8.25%</strong> combined sales tax rate.</p><p><strong>Step 1: Convert the percentage to a decimal</strong> 8.25% = 0.0825</p><p><strong>Step 2: Add 1 to the tax rate</strong> 1 + 0.0825 = 1.0825</p><p><strong>Step 3: Divide the total by this number</strong> $108.25 ÷ 1.0825 = <strong>$100.00</strong> (Pre-Tax Price)</p><p><strong>Step 4: Subtract to find the tax</strong> $108.25 − $100.00 = <strong>$8.25</strong> (Tax Amount)</p><p>So, the item cost $100.00 before tax, and you paid $8.25 in sales tax.</p><h2 id=\"reverse-sales-tax-by-state\">Reverse Sales Tax by State</h2><p>Sales tax rates vary widely across the United States. Here are examples of reverse tax calculations for a <strong>$100 total purchase</strong> in different states:</p><div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Combined Rate</th><th>Pre-Tax Price</th><th>Tax Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>California (avg)</td><td>8.82%</td><td>$91.89</td><td>$8.11</td></tr><tr><td>New York (avg)</td><td>8.52%</td><td>$92.15</td><td>$7.85</td></tr><tr><td>Texas (avg)</td><td>8.20%</td><td>$92.42</td><td>$7.58</td></tr><tr><td>Illinois (avg)</td><td>8.81%</td><td>$91.90</td><td>$8.10</td></tr><tr><td>Florida (avg)</td><td>7.02%</td><td>$93.44</td><td>$6.56</td></tr><tr><td>Georgia (avg)</td><td>7.35%</td><td>$93.16</td><td>$6.84</td></tr><tr><td>Washington (avg)</td><td>9.00%</td><td>$91.74</td><td>$8.26</td></tr><tr><td>Tennessee (avg)</td><td>9.55%</td><td>$91.28</td><td>$8.72</td></tr><tr><td>Oregon</td><td>0.00%</td><td>$100.00</td><td>$0.00</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Use our <a href=\"/sales-tax-calculator\">sales tax calculator</a> to find exact rates for any state or city.</p><h2 id=\"states-with-no-sales-tax\">States with No Sales Tax</h2><p>If you live in a state with no sales tax, reverse tax calculation is simple: the pre-tax price equals the total price. Five states have no state-level sales tax:</p><ol><li><strong>Oregon</strong> — No state sales tax</li><li><strong>Montana</strong> — No state sales tax</li><li><strong>New Hampshire</strong> — No state sales tax</li><li><strong>Delaware</strong> — No state sales tax</li><li><strong>Alaska</strong> — No state sales tax (but local cities may charge local sales tax)</li></ol><h2 id=\"how-to-use-a-reverse-tax-calculator\">How to Use a Reverse Tax Calculator</h2><ol><li><strong>Find your total amount</strong> — Look at your receipt or invoice for the grand total</li><li><strong>Know your sales tax rate</strong> — This includes state + local + special district taxes</li><li><strong>Enter the total and rate</strong> into the calculator</li><li><strong>Get your results</strong> — The calculator shows you the pre-tax price and the tax paid</li></ol><h3 id=\"finding-your-local-sales-tax-rate\">Finding Your Local Sales Tax Rate</h3><p>Sales tax isn't just the state rate — it's the combined rate of:</p><ul><li><strong>State sales tax</strong></li><li><strong>County sales tax</strong></li><li><strong>City sales tax</strong></li><li><strong>Special district taxes</strong> (transit, stadium, etc.)</li></ul><p>For example, in Chicago, Illinois:</p><ul><li>State: 6.25%</li><li>County: 1.75%</li><li>City: 1.25%</li><li>Special: 0.50%</li><li><strong>Total</strong>: 9.75%</li></ul><p>You can find your exact local rate using our <a href=\"/sales-tax-calculator\">sales tax calculator by state</a>.</p><h2 id=\"reverse-tax-for-businesses\">Reverse Tax for Businesses</h2><p>If you run a business, reverse tax calculations are essential for:</p><h3 id=\"1-sales-tax-filing\">1. Sales Tax Filing</h3><p>When filing your monthly or quarterly sales tax return, you need to report total sales and total tax collected. If your POS system tracks tax-inclusive totals, you'll need to reverse-calculate to separate the tax.</p><h3 id=\"2-expense-tracking\">2. Expense Tracking</h3><p>When you buy supplies for your business, receipts often show only the total. To deduct the actual expense (not the tax) on your Schedule C or corporate return, you need the pre-tax amount.</p><h3 id=\"3-pricing-strategy\">3. Pricing Strategy</h3><p>If you advertise \"tax-included\" pricing (common at festivals, food trucks, and some retail), you need to know how much tax is embedded in each sale to remit to the state.</p><h3 id=\"4-receipt-auditing\">4. Receipt Auditing</h3><p>If you're auditing receipts for accuracy, reverse tax calculation helps verify that the correct rate was applied.</p><h2 id=\"reverse-sales-tax-calculator-tool\">Reverse Sales Tax Calculator Tool</h2><p>Use this simple tool to calculate the pre-tax price from any total amount:</p><h3 id=\"quick-reference-table\">Quick Reference Table</h3><p>Here's a quick reference for a <strong>$100 total purchase</strong> at common tax rates:</p><div class=\"table-wrapper\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tax Rate</th><th>Pre-Tax Price</th><th>Tax Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5.00%</td><td>$95.24</td><td>$4.76</td></tr><tr><td>6.00%</td><td>$94.34</td><td>$5.66</td></tr><tr><td>6.25%</td><td>$94.12</td><td>$5.88</td></tr><tr><td>7.00%</td><td>$93.46</td><td>$6.54</td></tr><tr><td>7.25%</td><td>$93.24</td><td>$6.76</td></tr><tr><td>7.50%</td><td>$93.02</td><td>$6.98</td></tr><tr><td>8.00%</td><td>$92.59</td><td>$7.41</td></tr><tr><td>8.25%</td><td>$92.38</td><td>$7.62</td></tr><tr><td>8.50%</td><td>$92.17</td><td>$7.83</td></tr><tr><td>9.00%</td><td>$91.74</td><td>$8.26</td></tr><tr><td>9.50%</td><td>$91.32</td><td>$8.68</td></tr><tr><td>10.00%</td><td>$90.91</td><td>$9.09</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id=\"common-mistakes-in-reverse-tax-calculation\">Common Mistakes in Reverse Tax Calculation</h2><h3 id=\"mistake-1-subtracting-the-percentage\">Mistake 1: Subtracting the Percentage</h3><p><strong>Wrong</strong>: $108.25 − (8.25% of $108.25) = $99.33 <strong>Correct</strong>: $108.25 ÷ 1.0825 = $100.00</p><p>You cannot simply subtract the tax percentage from the total. You must divide by (1 + rate).</p><h3 id=\"mistake-2-using-the-wrong-rate\">Mistake 2: Using the Wrong Rate</h3><p>Make sure you're using the <strong>combined</strong> sales tax rate (state + local), not just the state rate. Using only the state rate will give you an incorrect pre-tax amount.</p><h3 id=\"mistake-3-rounding-errors\">Mistake 3: Rounding Errors</h3><p>Always calculate to at least 4 decimal places before rounding to cents. Rounding too early can cause penny-off discrepancies.</p><h2 id=\"frequently-asked-questions\">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p><strong>How do I calculate reverse sales tax manually?</strong> Divide the total price by (1 + tax rate as a decimal). For example, for a $50 total at 7% tax: $50 ÷ 1.07 = $46.73 pre-tax price.</p><p><strong>Can I use reverse tax for VAT (Value Added Tax)?</strong> Yes. The formula is the same. For example, with 20% VAT: £120 ÷ 1.20 = £100 pre-tax.</p><p><strong>Why doesn't subtracting the percentage work?</strong> Because the total includes tax, calculating the percentage of the total gives you a smaller number than the actual tax. The tax was calculated on the pre-tax price, not the total.</p><p><strong>What if I don't know my local tax rate?</strong> Use our <a href=\"/sales-tax-calculator\">sales tax calculator</a> to look up rates for any U.S. city or county.</p><p><strong>Are reverse tax calculations exact?</strong> Yes, mathematically. However, rounding to the nearest cent can sometimes cause a 1¢ difference. This is normal and acceptable for accounting purposes.</p><h2 id=\"related-resources\">Related Resources</h2><ul><li><a href=\"/sales-tax-calculator\">Sales Tax Calculator</a> — Calculate sales tax for any state</li><li><a href=\"/blog/sales-tax-by-state-guide-2026\">Sales Tax by State Guide</a> — Complete rate reference</li><li><a href=\"/property-tax-calculator\">Property Tax Calculator</a> — Estimate property tax</li><li><a href=\"/paycheck-calculator\">Paycheck Calculator</a> — Take-home pay after income tax</li></ul><h2 id=\"sources\">Sources</h2><ol><li>Tax Foundation — State and Local Sales Tax Rates (2026)</li><li>IRS — Publication 525 (Taxable and Nontaxable Income)</li><li>State Revenue Departments — Local Sales Tax Rate Tables</li><li>U.S. Census Bureau — Quarterly Summary of State and Local Tax Revenue</li></ol>10:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"mx-auto max-w-3xl lg:mx-0 lg:flex-1\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"prose-container\",\"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\":{\"__html\":\"$17\"}}]}]\n11:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-8\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"$L3\",null,{\"href\":\"/blog\",\"className\":\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors\",\"children\":\"← Back to Blog\"}]}]\n12:[\"$\",\"span\",\"SE tax 2026\",{\"className\":\"inline-flex items-center rounded-f